-=-=-=-
Priests:
The main advantage priests enjoy is that they can inherently tell if an
item is blessed or cursed, rather than having to wait to see if their
pet steps on it (which can take a long time, and isn't very effective
for sorting equipment after a larger battle) or lugging extra stuff to
an altar.  They also start with holy water, so they can bless stuff
easily; most other classes have to wait until they find (or convert) an
aligned altar to pray on.  Thus, if you find an early bag of holding or
magic lamp, you can take full advantage immediately; you can also bless
important scrolls (such as identify) before reading them.  As you can
tell from the starting mace and robe, priests can be good fighters and
spell casters, though they may never excell at either (and will
probably be better spell casters)--use your spells wisely, but don't be
afraid to bash a monster on the head when necessary.

The quest is largely populated by zombies and wraiths; #turn undead is
useful.  Every level except for the home level and the bottom level has
a graveyard, decreasing the chances that the wraiths will leave
corpses.  The filler levels are standard room-and-corridor, though many
of the rooms will be graveyards.  The quest home and the middle level
of the quest have unaligned altars; the middle level is a temple, with
a priest whom you can buy protection from, if you want.  The quest
nemesis, Nalzok, is a demon with two normal attacks (8-32 and 4-24
points of damage, respectively) and a magic attack.

The quest artifact is the Mitre of Holiness--an artifact helm of
brilliance.  In addition to the standard boosts to intelligence and
wisdom, it grants fire resistance (oh, yay; priests get that at level
20, and it's a fairly easy intrinsic to have by this point anyhow)
while carried, and halves damage from undead and demons, which is very
useful.  #Invoking the Mitre refills a large amount of your spell
energy.

Rangers:
As the name implies, rangers are designed to kill monsters at range.
They start with a bow and arrows, and their quest artifact is a bow.
They also get a cloak of displacement, which makes monsters attack
where they think the ranger is (rather than your actual location),
unless they're elven, in which case they get an elven cloak, which
isn't as useful.  Many players value displacement over invisibility, as
displacement works on all sighted monsters.  Unless you have
infravision, it can be difficult to see monsters approaching before
they're in melee range; one good option is to scare them (with a scroll
or Elbereth), and shoot them in the back while they're running away.
Let the knights be honorable, you're here to survive.  Another issue is
that arrows (and most other ranged weapons) will tend to disappear
after a while; blessing and enchanting them will help cut your losses.
Don't be afraid to keep some daggers as backup weapons.

The ranger quest is the only set of levels that reliably have trees.
Not that this makes much of a difference, but it's fun to note.  The
quest is populated primarily with centaurs, scorpions, and other
insects.  Oh, and there's a minotaur guarding the home area.  The upper
filler levels are forest, while the lower ones are caverns.  The quest
nemesis, Scorpius, is a giant scorpion (go figure).  His attacks are
fairly weak (two 2-12 damage attacks), but he can make you ill, which
will kill you after a very few moves.  Cure sickness or a blessed
unicorn horn will take care of this.  There's also a throne on the
bottom level, giving you an extra chance for a wish.

The quest artifact is the Longbow of Diana, which grants telepathy
while carried and reflection when wielded.  It also increases your
accuracy.  #Invoke it periodically to generate more arrows--you should
be able to amass a large stack of blessed, highly-enchanted arrows
fairly quickly.

Rogues:
In the beginning, there was Rogue.  And Rogue was good.  Which is why
we have NetHack.  However, rogues themselves are not good--to anyone
but themselves.  Rogues in NetHack are conniving little sneaks who will
take any advantage they can, and do so proudly.  And they're very good
at it, too.  They get a damage bonus when they attack a fleeing or
sleeping monster; they get alignment bonuses for stealing.  One of the
first things many rogues do is #dip their daggers into their potion of
sickness to poison them.  Daggers, unlike arrows and darts, don't
disappear when thrown (unless you throw them into lava, or some such;
there are also monsters which will eat daggers), so don't be afraid to
chuck a dagger whenever you can.

The monsters on the rogue quest can be bothersome--leprechauns will try
to steal your gold, and nymphs will take as much as they can, while
guardian nagas can destroy your armor.  Oh, and there're chameleons,
which can randomly polymorph into anything.  The filler levels are
normal room-and-corridor.  The middle and final levels have a fairly
nasty trick for the unprepared--there are rooms with no doors, and you
can't tunnel through the walls.  On the middle level, the stairs might
be in such a room; the quest nemesis definitely is.  You're a rogue;
surely you're not going to let a simple thing like a wall keep you from
your goal, are you?  Once you find him, the Master Assassin shouldn't
give too much trouble (two attacks, for 2-12 and 2-16 damage each; the
first one is poisoned).

The quest reward is the Master Key of Thievery, which makes the quest
challenge worthwhile.  In addition to being a key, it confers warning
and half physical damage; if you #invoke it, it'll untrap boxes and
doors flawlessly, though by this point your dexterity and luck are
probably high enough that you could do this regardless.  Like the Magic
Mirror of Merlin, if you apply or wield the Master Key, it'll tell you
things (guaranteed true if blessed).
